The benchmark indices recovered sharply to close well off the day's low on September 11, with the Nifty 50 falling 0.34 percent. Market breadth remained dominated by bears, with 2,006 shares declining against 1,220 advancing shares on the NSE. The market may rebound after the recent sharp correction, but questions remain over the sustainability of any recovery amid the broader bearish structure. Here are some short-term trading ideas to consider:
Rajesh Palviya, Senior Vice President Research (Head of Research) at Axis Securities
 Solara Active Pharma Sciences | CMP: Rs 749.15 
Solara Active Pharma Sciences has confirmed a bullish trend by breaking out above a key horizontal resistance zone of Rs 715-735, with a weekly close above Rs 715, backed by heavy trading volume that signals strong market participation. This upward momentum is further reinforced by buy signals on both the weekly and monthly Bollinger Bands, alongside favourable Relative Strength Index (RSI) readings across daily, weekly and monthly timeframes.
Furthermore, the price remains well above its upward-sloping 20-, 50-, 100- and 200-day simple moving averages (SMAs), solidifying sustained strength across all major timeframes.
 Aditya Birla Sun Life AMC | CMP: Rs 1,109.9 
Since April 2025, Aditya Birla Sun Life AMC has been trending higher within an “upward-sloping channel”, indicating a sustained uptrend. Recently, the stock recaptured its 20-, 50- and 100-day SMAs and is sustaining above them, confirming bullish sentiment.
The price action over the past couple of weeks shows that the stock has regained momentum from the channel’s lower-end support zone, indicating a sustained trend. The daily, weekly and monthly RSI is in favourable territory, indicating rising strength across all timeframes.
Driven by strong Friday buying and heavy trading volume, Yes Bank decisively broke past its six-week supply barrier at Rs 23 to close around Rs 24, signalling robust market participation. The rally gained traction following a firm bounce from its crucial 200-day simple moving average (SMA) support level at Rs 21.85, enabling the stock to reclaim its 20-, 50- and 100-day SMAs.
Broad-based strength is further reinforced by daily, weekly and monthly RSI readings sitting firmly in bullish territory, pointing to sustained momentum across all major timeframes.
 Hitesh Rathi, Technical Analyst (Equity & Derivatives) at Angel One 
The decline in Black Box over the past three months offers a lucrative opportunity to enter a technically uptrending stock, following a 150 percent rise in two months. The stock has also retested a key support and a prior breakout level, offering a favourable risk-reward proposition.
A bullish column reversal on its 3% x 3% Point and Figure charts points towards a cessation of the prior downswing and a resumption of the previous trend. Hence, buying is recommended around Rs 800-795.
 Adani Ports and Special Economic Zone | CMP: Rs 1,764.6 
The two-month retracement, accompanied by receding volumes, not only points towards an absence of any meaningful supply but also indicates that the decline was a retest of its prior price breakout zone and its 200-day SMA.
Adani Ports is now pointing towards a resumption of its prior uptrend and the cessation of its two-month downtrend, confirmed by the formation of a column reversal pattern on its Point and Figure chart.
A bullish column reversal pattern on its Relative Strength charts, when plotted against the Nifty 500 universe, accompanied by a new bullish Relative Strength breakout, points towards the emergence of new resilience in the stock. Hence, buying is recommended around Rs 1,765-1,760.
 Jatin Gedia, VP - Technical Research at Teji Mandi Investment Technologies 
Syrma SGS Technology has been in a long-term uptrend. Last week, it broke out from a bullish flag pattern, accompanied by above-average volume. Flags are trend continuation patterns, and we believe that the stock has started the next leg of its upmove.
The pullback occurred on very low volume, suggesting an accumulation phase. The momentum indicator has given a positive crossover, so this dip should be considered a buying opportunity.
On the upside, we expect the stock to rise towards Rs 1,713, the 100 percent Fibonacci extension level of the previous wave, with the potential to rise towards Rs 1,915, which is the 161.8 percent Fibonacci extension level.
