Technical bounce is expected in the market, especially after the severe selling pressure, but the sustainability of these gains will be key to watch amid elevated oil prices and US bond yields. According to experts, if the Nifty 50 extends its recovery, the 23,500-23,600 zone may act as a hurdle. On the downside, immediate support is seen at 23,200, followed by 23,000 as a crucial support level. Meanwhile, in case of further gains, the Bank Nifty may target the 57,000-57,200 hurdle zone, followed by 57,500-57,800. On the downside, the 56,200-56,000 zone could act as immediate support, followed by 55,700.
On September 11, the Nifty 50 dropped 80 points, or 0.34 percent, to 23,398, while the Bank Nifty rose 135 points, or 0.24 percent, to 56,607. However, market breadth remained in favour of the bears. About 2,006 shares were under pressure, compared with 1,220 shares that advanced on the NSE.
 Hitesh Rathi, Technical Analyst (Equity & Derivatives) at Angel One 
While the technical setup and trend continue to remain fragile and aligned to the downside, the recovery observed during the final trading session, coupled with prices approaching the crucial 23,200-23,000 support band, suggests that a near-term bounce cannot be ruled out. A multitude of factors underscore the possibility of an upcoming bounce.
Most technical indicators are now pointing towards oversold readings, reducing the likelihood of an extended decline from current levels. At the same time, breadth indicators, which track the overall health of the market, highlight the extent of the ongoing weakness, with more than 80 percent of stocks in the Nifty 50 basket and nearly 70 percent of stocks in the Nifty 500 currently in a downswing. In such a scenario, with prices approaching key support levels and market breadth showing significant deterioration, we anticipate a short-term bounce in the coming sessions.
That said, such a recovery should not be construed as a trend reversal. A meaningful reversal in the broader trend, at this point, still appears to be a far-fetched possibility. In terms of levels, the 23,450-23,500 band is likely to act as an immediate hurdle, followed by a stronger resistance zone at 23,600-23,650, which had previously acted as support.
On the downside, the 23,200 level, marked by the current weekly low, is likely to provide an immediate cushion, followed by stronger support in the 23,100-23,000 band. This zone is significant not only as a prior swing low but also as a key psychological support level.
 Strategy : Sell Nifty Futures near 23,500, with a stop-loss of 23,650 and book profits near 23,300-23,250.
 Rajesh Palviya, Senior Vice President Research (Head of Research) at Axis Securities 
Nifty extended its losing streak for a fifth consecutive week, closing down nearly 500 points on a week-on-week basis. However, Friday's session witnessed strong buying interest, with the index staging a sharp recovery from the lows, indicating renewed demand and buying interest at lower levels.
On the weekly chart, the index formed a bearish candle after breaking below the 23,800-23,600 support zone, although Friday's recovery suggests buying interest at lower levels and tempers the immediate downside pressure.
Immediate support is placed at 23,100-23,000, which has held well so far; a break below this zone could open the door towards 22,800. On the upside, 23,600 is the first hurdle; a sustained move above it could shift the tone constructively towards 24,000-24,100.
The weekly RSI remains below its reference line, reflecting some underlying weakness, although it is not in oversold territory yet. Broadly, the index remains in a pattern of lower highs and lower lows, so caution is still warranted. That said, Friday's bounce from 23,231 is a constructive sign; confirmation would require a move back above 23,600, opening the way towards 23,800-24,000.
